    "intro": "Driving out to look at every junk pile before quoting it costs a lot. An hour of drive time and walking the job can eat the profit on a small load, and the customer often books someone else while waiting for you. Photo estimates fix most of that. The customer sends pictures, you give a price range, and the crew confirms the final price on site. Done well, photo quoting gets accurate numbers to customers within the hour and still protects you from the pile that turns out to be twice as big as it looked. This post covers what to ask for, how to read volume from a photo, how to phrase the range, and how to handle the job when it is not what the photos showed.",
